<p>Rogers is a small town in eastern Bell County on US-190, out in the farm and ranch country east of Temple. The town has deep agricultural roots, and many properties around it have more land and more buildings than a typical city lot. We drive out from Temple along US-190, so Rogers is part of our regular service area.</p>

<h2>Rural Properties Mean More Than One Roof</h2>
<p>When a Rogers homeowner calls about a roof, the conversation often includes the barn, a hay shed, an equipment cover or a shop. Each building has its own needs.</p>

<h3>Farmhouses and older homes</h3>
<p>Older farmhouses may have been re-roofed several times, sometimes with layers stacked on top of each other or with metal installed over old shingles. Before a replacement, we check what is underneath and whether the decking and framing are sound. Porches, additions and lean-tos built at different times often meet the main roof at awkward angles that need careful flashing.</p>

<h3>Homes in town</h3>
<p>In-town homes are mostly single-story houses with asphalt shingle or metal roofs. Common issues include sun-worn shingles, failed pipe boots, and gutters that cannot keep up with heavy rain.</p>

<h3>Barns, shops and outbuildings</h3>
<p>Metal is the standard for agricultural buildings, usually exposed-fastener panels. These roofs last a long time if they are maintained, but fasteners loosen, washers crack, and panels rust at laps and cut edges. Wind can catch a loose panel and peel it back, and once one goes, the ones next to it often follow. Tightening or replacing fasteners and securing trim are inexpensive ways to prevent bigger damage. Our <a href="/services/metal-roofing-temple-tx/">metal roofing</a> service covers repairs and new metal roofs for homes and outbuildings.</p>

<h2>Weather on Open Farmland</h2>
<p>The Blackland Prairie east of Temple is wide open, with few trees or buildings to slow storms. Straight-line winds and hail can sweep across fields and hit every building on a property. Spring brings the most severe weather, with a second round possible in the fall. Summer heat bakes metal and shingles alike, and sudden downpours test every seam and gutter.</p>
<div class="callout callout--warn">Don't climb onto a barn or shop roof to check for damage. Old metal panels can be slick, sharp and too weak to walk on safely. Take photos from the ground and call for an inspection.</div>

